WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:36.380
Hello, my name is Zisl Slepovitch

00:00:36.630 --> 00:00:38.759
and I'm musician-in-residence at Yale

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:40.890
University's Fortunoff Video Archive

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:42.549
for Holocaust Testimonies.

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:45.630
This video series is designed as a companion

00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:47.790
to our album, Where Is Our Homeland,

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:50.178
which is the first volume in the Archive's

00:00:50.310 --> 00:00:51.829
ongoing effort to present songs

00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:54.959
and poems performed by survivors in

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:57.539
testimonies given to the Fortunoff Archive

00:00:57.720 --> 00:00:59.499
over the last 40 years.

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:01.889
You can learn more about the project on the

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:05.620
Archive's website, songsfromtestimonies.org.

00:01:06.510 --> 00:01:07.859
Where Is Our Homeland?

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:08.987
Songs from Testimonies.

00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:12.329
Vol. One was recorded in 2019

00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:14.508
by the Zisl Slepovitch Ensemble, featuring

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:16.890
renowned singer Sasha Lurje,

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:20.099
Craig Judelman on the violin, Joshua

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:22.349
Camp on the accordion, keyboards and

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:24.569
guitar, Dmitry Ishenko on the

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:26.819
bass, and myself on woodwinds.

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:29.909
The songs in this collection differ vastly

00:01:29.910 --> 00:01:31.870
in terms of style, social and historical

00:01:32.430 --> 00:01:34.739
background, language, and many other

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:37.199
aspects, but collectively they

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:38.964
create a multidimensional picture of

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:41.195
Jewish life before and during World

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:43.030
War II.

00:01:44.510 --> 00:01:47.000
Several songs that we are presenting here

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:49.129
were actually the beginning of this music

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:51.115
project carried out by the archive,

00:01:52.310 --> 00:01:54.200
and they all came from

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:56.810
Liubov' K.'s testimony.

00:01:57.860 --> 00:01:58.860
Liubov' K.

00:01:59.090 --> 00:02:01.489
was born and raised and lived her life

00:02:01.490 --> 00:02:03.829
in Zvenigorodka, in Ukraine,

00:02:04.370 --> 00:02:06.769
and she ended up with many

00:02:06.770 --> 00:02:07.848
of her fellow citizens

00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:11.179
in a slave labor camp

00:02:12.170 --> 00:02:14.480
suffering hunger,

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:16.910
cold, torture,

00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:20.930
loss of family, friends

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:22.129
and homes.

00:02:23.570 --> 00:02:25.789
Two of her four songs that she

00:02:25.790 --> 00:02:26.868
presented did not have

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:30.169
any music.

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:32.050
She recited them as poems.

00:02:32.690 --> 00:02:34.258
So I took liberty to write music

00:02:35.330 --> 00:02:37.250
to those poems

00:02:37.580 --> 00:02:39.491
in the style that would be adequate and

00:02:39.830 --> 00:02:42.590
representative of the music

00:02:42.830 --> 00:02:45.379
popular at the time in Eastern Europe

00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:46.703
and in the Soviet Union, in

00:02:47.810 --> 00:02:49.030
Ukraine possibly.

00:02:50.390 --> 00:02:51.566
And this song that tells

00:02:52.610 --> 00:02:53.838
the story of Stepan,

00:02:54.980 --> 00:02:57.830
the Ukrainian collaborationist guard,

00:02:58.610 --> 00:03:00.649
an extremely cruel person.

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:04.500
I use the genres of tango,

00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:06.480
which was a very

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:09.149
young genre at the time, and

00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:10.510
military march.

00:03:10.770 --> 00:03:12.387
I sometimes turn to military feel

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:14.200
there.

00:03:19.260 --> 00:03:22.580
Stepan "the Blond," a guard so strict.

00:03:22.940 --> 00:03:25.500
Day in, day out, with his rifle he sits.

00:03:25.780 --> 00:03:28.420
Dutifully doing his duties,

00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:31.580
Law and Order are his domain.

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:34.420
But this gang of ours is very naughty.

00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:37.220
They don’t want to listen to their Blond.

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:40.260
He only has to turn his back,

00:03:40.580 --> 00:03:43.500
Off to town (to beg for
food) they run like rabbits.

00:03:44.260 --> 00:03:46.860
So once, on their day off, this brave Ten

00:03:47.140 --> 00:03:49.580
Went running to the village.

00:03:49.940 --> 00:03:52.700
When the Blond learned
of it at the inspection

00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:55.780
He decided to teach them a little lesson.

00:03:56.340 --> 00:03:58.900
When they returned, the Blond was waiting:

00:03:59.180 --> 00:04:01.900
“Where have you been,
who gave you permission?”

00:04:02.140 --> 00:04:04.700
“We beg: forgive us,
it won’t happen again.”

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:08.380
“I shall forgive,” he said,
but thought: “Just you wait.”

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:11.620
The next day he got his Ten together,

00:04:11.900 --> 00:04:14.340
And brought them to
the German to be judged.

00:04:14.660 --> 00:04:17.620
“We are lost, doomed, never to return!

00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:20.860
For a piece of bread we’ll meet our end.”

00:04:21.620 --> 00:04:24.420
But, alas, every one of them returned,

00:04:24.740 --> 00:04:27.380
And a delightful treat awaited them.

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:31.420
The Blond prepared a tasty breakfast

00:04:31.660 --> 00:04:35.060
For each of his Ten — 25 hard ones.

00:04:35.460 --> 00:04:39.060
The ramrod whipped, and the moans wheezed.

00:04:39.420 --> 00:04:41.780
And our gang went down in tears and terror.

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:45.300
To the side the Blond was
sitting, smiling, satisfied,

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:48.420
Having taught these naughty
Ten such a tidy lesson.

