00:07:33.580 --> 00:07:35.060
A month after Liubov' K.

00:07:35.300 --> 00:07:36.980
and her fellow prisoners were taken from

00:07:37.140 --> 00:07:38.900
the Zvenigorodka ghetto to the Nemorozh

00:07:39.060 --> 00:07:40.830
labor camp north of the city,

00:07:41.370 --> 00:07:43.330
conditions in the camp became so crowded

00:07:43.920 --> 00:07:46.076
that the German authorities decided to split

00:07:46.170 --> 00:07:47.170
the camp in two.

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:50.909
Liubov', and half of the Nemorozh prisoners

00:07:50.910 --> 00:07:52.820
were transferred to Smiltchytsi,

00:07:53.060 --> 00:07:54.739
a village approximately 12

00:07:54.740 --> 00:07:57.220
kilometers northwest of Nemorozh.

00:07:58.060 --> 00:08:00.620
Later that year, on November 2,

00:08:00.740 --> 00:08:03.380
1942, the German gendarmerie

00:08:03.540 --> 00:08:05.399
liquidated the Nemorozh camp,

00:08:05.790 --> 00:08:07.456
executing all of the prisoners who

00:08:08.040 --> 00:08:09.040
remained there.

00:08:10.020 --> 00:08:12.078
Liubov' K. and the other prisoners who had

00:08:12.120 --> 00:08:14.080
been transferred to Smiltchytsi remained

00:08:14.850 --> 00:08:17.340
there until December 1942,

00:08:17.910 --> 00:08:19.968
when they were transferred yet again, this

00:08:20.280 --> 00:08:21.652
time to Budyshche, a village

00:08:22.650 --> 00:08:24.530
eight kilometers northeast of Smiltchysti.

00:08:26.370 --> 00:08:28.649
Conditions in Budyshche were somewhat

00:08:28.650 --> 00:08:30.080
better than Nemorozh and Smiltchytsi

00:08:31.260 --> 00:08:32.679
for two main reasons.

00:08:33.300 --> 00:08:35.549
First, the prisoners were housed

00:08:35.550 --> 00:08:38.339
in a village clubhouse
rather than in barns.

00:08:38.760 --> 00:08:40.740
As Liubov' recalls, "The

00:08:40.940 --> 00:08:43.380
building wasn't heated, but the floor

00:08:43.500 --> 00:08:45.860
was wooden instead of dirt, and

00:08:46.020 --> 00:08:49.260
at least it was an enclosed space."

00:08:50.310 --> 00:08:53.060
Second, the villagers of Budyshche

00:08:53.220 --> 00:08:55.460
and the nearby village of Shesteryntsi

00:08:55.740 --> 00:08:57.798
were remarkably generous towards the labor

00:08:58.080 --> 00:08:59.190
camp prisoners.

00:08:59.790 --> 00:09:02.100
Liubov' recalls, "They were

00:09:02.300 --> 00:09:03.540
wonderful people.

00:09:03.660 --> 00:09:05.860
All we can do is say thank you to them for

00:09:06.060 --> 00:09:07.350
the rest of our lives.

00:09:07.920 --> 00:09:10.169
Of course, we would like to express our

00:09:10.170 --> 00:09:11.879
gratitude to them somehow.

00:09:12.690 --> 00:09:14.609
They would come to the camp, they would

00:09:14.610 --> 00:09:16.919
persuade the politsai, bring us

00:09:16.920 --> 00:09:19.350
half liters, bring us pieces of bread,

00:09:19.590 --> 00:09:20.970
a potato here and there.

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:23.271
And if they lived nearby, something hot

00:09:23.580 --> 00:09:24.580
to eat.

00:09:24.630 --> 00:09:27.060
They really supported us."

00:09:28.260 --> 00:09:30.569
As time went on, the prisoners themselves

00:09:30.570 --> 00:09:31.769
became more daring.

00:09:32.280 --> 00:09:34.338
On their way back to the camp after a hard

00:09:34.590 --> 00:09:36.740
day's labor on the Transit Highway IV

00:09:37.050 --> 00:09:38.618
project, they would run in pairs

00:09:39.750 --> 00:09:41.909
into the village to beg for something to

00:09:41.910 --> 00:09:42.910
eat.

00:09:43.440 --> 00:09:45.659
It was one such foray into a

00:09:45.660 --> 00:09:47.473
neighboring village that inspired the

00:09:47.880 --> 00:09:50.129
song "Stepan." As

00:09:50.130 --> 00:09:52.529
Liubov' recalls, in general,

00:09:52.650 --> 00:09:54.820
the Ukrainian auxiliary police who

00:09:54.980 --> 00:09:57.259
guarded the camp would turn a blind

00:09:57.260 --> 00:10:00.289
eye to the prisoner's brief absences.

00:10:00.800 --> 00:10:02.900
As Liubov' puts it, "The

00:10:03.060 --> 00:10:05.260
politsai had been with us so long, they

00:10:05.420 --> 00:10:07.780
knew that we wouldn't run away."

00:10:09.200 --> 00:10:11.111
While these temporary absences could be

00:10:11.420 --> 00:10:13.780
overlooked, any actual escape

00:10:13.980 --> 00:10:16.300
would entail severe consequences

00:10:16.420 --> 00:10:18.380
for the remaining prisoners in the camp.

00:10:19.390 --> 00:10:21.340
The German overseers had a rule

00:10:21.540 --> 00:10:23.910
that if one prisoner ran
away from the camp,

00:10:24.180 --> 00:10:26.469
10 innocent prisoners would be shot.

00:10:27.180 --> 00:10:29.287
This threat proved to be a strong deterrent

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:31.980
against any plans of escape.

00:10:32.790 --> 00:10:34.860
As Liubov' puts it, "We

00:10:35.020 --> 00:10:36.900
didn't want to be the cause of others'

00:10:37.100 --> 00:10:38.100
death.

00:10:38.120 --> 00:10:40.340
We decided that whatever happened, it

00:10:40.500 --> 00:10:43.260
would happen to all of us together."

00:10:44.610 --> 00:10:47.309
So despite the guards generally permissive

00:10:47.310 --> 00:10:50.130
attitude towards temporary absences,

00:10:50.640 --> 00:10:52.890
on one unfortunate Sunday,

00:10:53.340 --> 00:10:55.799
Liubov' and nine other prisoners snuck

00:10:55.800 --> 00:10:57.889
out to the village during a roll call.

00:10:58.860 --> 00:11:00.940
The blond guard Stepan met them at

00:11:01.100 --> 00:11:03.619
the gate as they returned, demanding

00:11:03.620 --> 00:11:04.929
to know where they had been.

00:11:05.840 --> 00:11:08.020
The prisoners apologized profusely

00:11:08.180 --> 00:11:10.220
and promised not to venture out again.

00:11:11.240 --> 00:11:13.340
Much to their surprise, Stepan didn't

00:11:13.500 --> 00:11:15.420
punish anyone immediately.

00:11:16.170 --> 00:11:18.521
Instead, he waited until the following day,

00:11:18.870 --> 00:11:19.870
Monday.

00:11:20.520 --> 00:11:22.409
During that morning's roll call, he

00:11:22.410 --> 00:11:25.140
demanded, "the ten of you, step forward."

00:11:26.190 --> 00:11:28.199
While everyone else was taken out to work

00:11:28.470 --> 00:11:30.820
in the forest that day, Stepan

00:11:30.940 --> 00:11:33.339
took his ten unfortunate prisoners

00:11:33.520 --> 00:11:34.520
to Lysyanka.

00:11:35.650 --> 00:11:37.806
The group was fully
convinced that they were

00:11:37.870 --> 00:11:40.299
being taken to the gendarmerie to be shot.

00:11:41.020 --> 00:11:43.389
But instead, Stepan
took them to the highway

00:11:43.390 --> 00:11:45.500
department in Lysyanka, where

00:11:45.700 --> 00:11:47.769
the director and foreman of the highway

00:11:47.770 --> 00:11:48.850
department worked.

00:11:49.930 --> 00:11:52.479
There they were greeted by a young mean

00:11:52.480 --> 00:11:54.219
German by the name of Hans.

00:11:54.940 --> 00:11:57.399
Hans ordered Stepan to give

00:11:57.400 --> 00:11:59.649
them each twenty lashes with a

00:11:59.650 --> 00:12:00.650
ramrod.

00:12:01.030 --> 00:12:03.070
And that is exactly what Stepan did.

00:12:03.940 --> 00:12:04.959
The following day.

00:12:05.140 --> 00:12:07.569
Tuesday, Liubov' and the other

00:12:07.570 --> 00:12:10.029
nine prisoners ate their usual breakfast,

00:12:10.120 --> 00:12:12.180
watery gruel, and then one

00:12:12.380 --> 00:12:14.620
by one Stepan and one other

00:12:14.780 --> 00:12:17.540
guard gave them each twenty five lashes

00:12:17.860 --> 00:12:19.460
with a ramrod in the kitchen.

00:12:20.410 --> 00:12:22.660
As Liubov' recalls, "We

00:12:22.780 --> 00:12:25.539
crawled out of the kitchen on all fours,

00:12:26.110 --> 00:12:28.419
and after that meeting we went back to

00:12:28.420 --> 00:12:30.020
work."

00:12:31.240 --> 00:12:34.319
To immortalize this agonizing experience,

00:12:34.320 --> 00:12:36.599
the prisoners wrote a song describing the

00:12:36.600 --> 00:12:39.109
brutality of handsome, yet heartless

00:12:39.120 --> 00:12:40.120
Stepan.

00:12:40.560 --> 00:12:42.184
Like the other three songs that Liubov'

00:12:42.510 --> 00:12:44.979
shares in her testimony, this ode

00:12:44.980 --> 00:12:47.500
to Stepan was composed collectively

00:12:47.700 --> 00:12:48.860
by the prisoners.

00:12:49.740 --> 00:12:51.120
As Liubov' explains,

00:12:52.290 --> 00:12:54.599
"Everyone wrote the songs all together.

00:12:55.200 --> 00:12:57.258
This one would give a word, that one would

00:12:57.300 --> 00:12:58.300
give two.

00:12:58.560 --> 00:13:00.659
This one, a line, and the next would add

00:13:00.660 --> 00:13:02.999
another. That's how it came together for

00:13:03.000 --> 00:13:05.140
us." While the lyrics of

00:13:05.260 --> 00:13:06.975
this song described their ordeal in

00:13:07.390 --> 00:13:09.700
unflinchingly factual terms,

00:13:10.180 --> 00:13:11.895
in a certain sense, by writing this

00:13:12.520 --> 00:13:14.780
song, the prisoners were able to have

00:13:14.900 --> 00:13:17.289
the last laugh at Stepan's expense.

00:13:18.020 --> 00:13:20.078
He tried to break their will, and yet they

00:13:20.270 --> 00:13:22.819
still had the power to compose a song

00:13:22.820 --> 00:13:24.620
about their blond tormentor.

00:13:25.670 --> 00:13:27.777
Because songs like these were not committed

00:13:27.980 --> 00:13:30.300
to paper, but instead were memorized,

00:13:30.830 --> 00:13:33.619
if even one of the camp inmates

00:13:33.620 --> 00:13:35.839
survived, their collective

00:13:35.840 --> 00:13:38.449
history would survive as well, along

00:13:38.450 --> 00:13:40.790
with the identity of their tormentors.

00:13:42.290 --> 00:13:44.449
While Liubov' does not shy away from

00:13:44.450 --> 00:13:46.399
describing the cruelty of Ukrainian

00:13:46.400 --> 00:13:48.220
collaborators like Stepan,

00:13:48.380 --> 00:13:50.869
her testimony also pays grateful

00:13:50.870 --> 00:13:53.510
tribute to the local Ukrainian villagers

00:13:53.810 --> 00:13:56.029
who brought food to Liubov' and her fellow

00:13:56.030 --> 00:13:58.780
prisoners while they were in the Budyshche

00:13:59.020 --> 00:14:00.020
labor camp.

00:14:00.820 --> 00:14:02.900
Complex accounts of collaboration

00:14:03.140 --> 00:14:05.509
and compassion are just one of the

00:14:05.510 --> 00:14:08.089
aspects that make survivor testimonies

00:14:08.090 --> 00:14:10.740
like Liubov's completely

00:14:10.940 --> 00:14:12.704
invaluable in preserving the history

00:14:12.860 --> 00:14:14.180
of the Holocaust.

